+  What I built
+
+  A single TaskQueue class in src/index.ts (named export, zero runtime dependencies) covering all 12 phases. The implementation is unified around one Task model and a per-queue state record, so later phases compose
+  cleanly with earlier ones.
+
+  The key design decisions that the marathon's later phases force on the earlier ones:
+
+  - Sort order is priority → availableAt → FIFO-sequence, not availableAt-first as Phase 4's prose suggests. Phase 5's "retry preserves priority" test re-enqueues a high-priority task with a later availableAt (due to
+  backoff) yet still expects it to win over a low-priority task — so priority must dominate. A monotonic seq counter guarantees stable FIFO even when timestamps collide.
+  - attempts increments only on dequeue (0 → 1 on first pull), so the backoff backoffMs * 2^(attempts-1) and the attempts <= maxRetries retry gate fall out naturally (maxRetries: 0 → no retry → straight to DLQ).
+  - No real timers for delays/retries/cron — availability is purely availableAt <= Date.now() re-checked at dequeue, which is exactly what vi.advanceTimersByTime drives. The only real setTimeout is Phase 12's shutdown
+  force-cancel deadline.
+  - Dependencies are filtered at dequeue time, so completing a blocker auto-unblocks dependents with no bookkeeping; DFS cycle detection runs at enqueue.
+  - Shutdown tracks the in-flight set, resolves early when the last task completes (via a hook in complete()), force-cancels the remainder on timeout, and is idempotent by caching the promise.
+
+  I also fixed one self-inflicted bug before it shipped: a JSDoc comment originally contained the literal */N cron token, whose */ would have prematurely closed the block comment and broken compilation.
+
+  The only file changed is src/index.ts (the starting .gitkeep is untouched).
